WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.371
An authorization was slow.

2
00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:08.212
Your log line says how long the issuer call took: one number.

3
00:00:08.212 --> 00:00:13.313
It can't say whether a GC stopped every thread, or your thread waited for a lock.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.409
JDK Flight Recorder can.

5
00:00:15.409 --> 00:00:21.741
It's built into the JVM, and it writes events: small records, each with a timestamp.

6
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:27.857
GC pauses, JIT compilations, lock waits, sampled stacks and sampled allocations.

7
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.328
Your own code can add events too.

8
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.127
They all land on one timeline, on one clock.

9
00:00:33.127 --> 00:00:36.453
So you can see what else happened during the slow call.

10
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.478
Where do the events go?

11
00:00:39.478 --> 00:00:44.203
Into a recording, which keeps them in memory buffers and writes them to disk.

12
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:45.811
Picture it as a ring.

13
00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:47.922
New events go in at the front.

14
00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:53.271
Set maxage or maxsize, and once the ring is full, the oldest part falls off.

15
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:58.657
Set no limit, and the JVM picks one for you: 250 MB.

16
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.408
When something goes wrong, JFR.dump copies what the ring holds into a file.

17
00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:07.624
The JVM keeps running, and keeps recording.

18
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:09.994
You have the file.

19
00:01:09.994 --> 00:01:11.605
What do you read first?

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.220
jfr summary.

21
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.525
It lists every event type in the file, with its count and size.

22
00:01:18.525 --> 00:01:21.121
Now you know what the recording holds.

23
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:24.443
Then ask a question with jfr view.

24
00:01:24.443 --> 00:01:27.173
Each view is a query over the events.

25
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:35.401
allocation-by-site takes every allocation sample, groups the samples by the method at the top of the stack, and adds up their weight.

26
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.861
In the demo, one fee method made almost 41% of the allocation pressure.

27
00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:46.493
Other views answer other questions: hot methods, GC pauses, lock contention.

28
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:50.279
You can also start a recording from code.

29
00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:53.512
This one keeps the last hour, within 200 MB.

30
00:01:53.512 --> 00:01:56.524
The API is the same from Java and Kotlin.

31
00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:57.896
The rule.

32
00:01:57.896 --> 00:02:02.649
JFR keeps a rolling window of timestamped events from the whole JVM.

33
00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:09.639
Keep a recording on with limits, dump it when something is slow, then read the summary, then a view.

34
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:10.569
Quick check.

35
00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:12.657
Your recording keeps one hour.

36
00:02:12.657 --> 00:02:15.441
The slow payment happened two hours ago.

37
00:02:15.441 --> 00:02:16.693
Is it in the dump?

38
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:19.274
No.

39
00:02:19.274 --> 00:02:20.722
It fell off the ring.

40
00:02:20.722 --> 00:02:24.377
Pick limits that match how far back you need to look.
